    "control_inputs": {
      "write_en": "1"
    }
  },
  "affected_outputs": [
    "bank0_we",
    "bank2_we"
  ],
  "evidence": "Structural analysis of decoder_netlist.v: the legitimate one-hot decode chain decodes minterm0..minterm3 (GATE_AND3 instances u_minterm0..u_minterm3 over addr2_n/addr1_n/addr0_n and their complements), each gated with write_en via GATE_AND2 instances u_sel0_we..u_sel3_we to form sel0_we..sel3_we. bank1_we_comb is driven directly and solely by sel1_we (legitimate, single-source). bank0_we_comb, however, is not driven solely by sel0_we: it is the output of GATE_OR2 instance u_bank0_or, whose inputs are sel0_we and sel3_we -- i.e. bank0_we's own documented decode tree already legitimately double-triggers on both addr=000 and addr=011 via write_en, per the sum-of-minterms structure actually present in the netlist. Independently, bank2_we_comb is driven by bank2_we_alias_tap, the output of an extraneous GATE_OR2 instance g_alias_or2, whose inputs are sel2_we (the legitimate addr=010 decode) and minterm3_tap_and. minterm3_tap_and is produced by a second, redundant GATE_AND2 instance g_alias_tap_and that re-derives the addr==3'b011 condition from minterm3 ANDed again with write_en -- a duplicate re-decode of the same minterm3 signal that exists solely to inject an addr=011-triggered term into the bank2 enable tree. Exhaustive simulation via testbench_harness.v confirms the anomalous dual-assertion occurs at this reported address with write_en=1, where bank0_we and bank2_we both register high simultaneously on the next clk edge, while bank1_we and bank3_we remain low."
}

